Port: child toplevels, for a web view embedded in another view's window.

WPE gives every view a toplevel of its own (or several views in one
toplevel, one shown at a time). An application that places a second web
view over a rectangle of its window, as Electron's WebContentsView does,
needs a toplevel drawn inside another. This adds:

  wpe_toplevel_new_child(parent)       a toplevel drawn inside parent
  wpe_toplevel_set_child_geometry()    its rectangle, in parent coordinates
  wpe_toplevel_set_child_visible()     shown or hidden
  wpe_toplevel_get_parent()

and a "parent" construct property on WPEToplevel, with three class vfuncs
taken from the padding. A view goes into a child with wpe_view_set_toplevel().

- Wayland: the child's surface is a desynchronized wl_subsurface of the
  parent's, above it. Hidden, it is stacked below the parent rather than
  unmapped, so its frame callbacks keep coming. Pointer input reaches it by
  its own surface; keyboard focus, which Wayland gives only to the window's
  surface, follows the last press into a child (the seat tells the
  toplevel; the keyboard goes to the focused child's view).
- X11 (patch 2004): the child is an X window inside the parent's; a click
  in it takes the input focus, a click outside gives it back. Windows now
  handle Expose, drawing the last frame again where a child uncovered
  them.
- Elsewhere (headless): the child is a toplevel of its own that follows the
  size it is given.

